menu "Netserver configuration"

    config NETSERVER_WIFI_SSID
        string "Access point Wi-Fi name"
        default "ProductivityWire"
        help
            Name of the Wi-Fi network hosted by the ESP32 (1 to 32 bytes).

    config NETSERVER_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "Access point Wi-Fi password"
        default "HelloWorld123"
        help
            Use 8 to 63 bytes for WPA2, or leave empty for an open network.

    config NETSERVER_WIFI_CHANNEL
        int "Access point Wi-Fi channel"
        range 1 11
        default 1

    config NETSERVER_SERVER_PORT
        int "Client machine's server port"
        range 1 65535
        default 3000
        help
            The first client to join the access point is selected as the server.
            Its DHCP address is used as http://<client IP>:<port>.
            This port must expose /health and /api/triggers, either directly from
            FastAPI or through a proxy. Bind the server to 0.0.0.0, not localhost.
            No web server or control page is hosted on the ESP32.
            When the selected client disconnects, reporting stops and the next
            new connection can become the server. Other clients already connected
            must reconnect to become the selected server.

    config NETSERVER_SERVER_RETRY_MS
        int "Server readiness retry interval (milliseconds)"
        range 1000 60000
        default 5000
        help
            Retry /health until the selected client's API responds. A valid HTTP
            Date header synchronizes the clock; Uvicorn sends this by default.
            Reporting arms automatically once the server and clock are ready.

    config NETSERVER_DEVICE_ID
        string "Sensor device ID"
        default "tripwire-01"
        help
            Must match a key in the server's DEVICE_HOSTS mapping.
            Use 1 to 100 ASCII letters, digits, '.', '_' or '-'.

    config NETSERVER_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Server request timeout (milliseconds)"
        range 500 10000
        default 3000
        help
            Triggers are queued and sent by a background task while armed.
            Requests are not automatically retried, because the server does not
            deduplicate events and retries could switch the workspace twice.

endmenu
